      "pageDescription": "A Belgrade court officially placed Alta Banka into bankruptcy, triggering state-backed payouts for depositors following the arrests of three bank officials for an alleged $427 million heist.",
      "evidenceText": "Serbian Bank Tied to Outgoing Regime Collapses After Internal Vault Theft | OCCRP Corruption • Serbia Serbian Bank Tied to Outgoing Regime Collapses After Internal Vault Theft A Belgrade court officially placed Alta Banka into bankruptcy, triggering state-backed payouts for depositors following the arrests of three bank officials for an alleged $427 million heist. Banner: Андрей Романенко/ Wikimedia Commons | Alta bank building in Belgrade, Serbia. Reported by Zdravko Ljubas OCCRP November 3, 2026 | 3 min read Share Article &times; Aa Text Settings &times; Text Size Small Medium Large Line Spacing Compact Normal Spacious A Serbian commercial court officially launched bankruptcy proceedings against Belgrade-based Alta Banka on Tuesday and activated a state mechanism to refund depositors, just one day after top executives were arrested for allegedly stealing more than 45.5 billion Serbian dinars (more than $427 million) from the institution's vault. The financial collapse stems from a massive alleged internal theft. In coordinated statements on Monday, the Public Prosecutor's Office for Organized Crime and the Interior Ministry confirmed the detention of the \"responsible person\" of Alta Banka, identified by authorities only by the initials D.M. (born 1981). The Serbian branch of regional N1 TV subsequently identified the suspect as Davor Macura. Also arrested were the director of the treasury and cash management department, G.M. (born 1987), and a treasury associate, I.P. (born 1982). Macura is suspected of abusing his position to illegally extract the funds, defrauding Alta Banka of exactly 45,556,596,250 dinars—an amount worth more than $427 million—according to the Interior Ministry and the Public Prosecutor’s Office for Organized Crime. Prosecutors allege that prior to October 31, the two treasury officials provided Macura with access cards or physically accompanied him to allow unauthorized entry into the bank's vault. Once inside, he allegedly removed the cash, the prosecutor's office stated. The arrests followed joint operations by the criminal police and units combating organized and financial crime, which searched the bank's premises and Macura's residence on October 31 and November 1. Macura’s defense attorney confirmed to N1 that his client was held in police custody and would be questioned by organized crime prosecutors after noon on Tuesday. The financial collapse and arrests come less than two weeks after Serbia's ruling party, led by former Serbia's president Aleksandar Vučić, was defeated in the October 25 parliamentary elections by the Students' Movement. Following Macura's arrest, the Belgrade-based independent magazine Vreme published an article characterizing Alta Banka as the financial core of Vučić's outgoing administration. According to Vreme, the state artificially fueled Alta Banka's rapid rise by transferring lucrative public contracts to the private institution—including state utility and parking payments—and installing its ATMs in police stations. Speaking to the Serbian broadcaster B92, Vučić described the situation as \"complicated\" and called on authorities to quickly question all relevant actors to determine what caused the bank's collapse. \"I assume the people in the National Bank wanted to protect themselves and acted in accordance with regulations, I don't doubt that,\" Vučić said. \"But to me, all of this is a bit strange.\" He questioned the timing of the central bank's intervention, stating it was unclear why officials did not wait another week before making decisions on an issue of such \"great importance for the state.\" The court's ruling to place the bank into bankruptcy formalizes Monday's decision by the National Bank of Serbia to revoke Alta Banka's operating license. Prior to the central bank's intervention, Alta Banka had confirmed the impending bankruptcy, stating that authorities were taking the necessary steps to protect creditors. The central bank also advised corporate clients to consult the Ministry of Finance regarding their fiscal obligations during the closure. The Commercial Court in Belgrade appointed Serbia's Deposit Insurance Agency as the bankruptcy trustee. The agency announced Tuesday it will begin executing state-backed payouts for insured depositors—covering accounts up to a legal limit of 50,000 euros ($55,906)—within the next seven working days, assuring the public it has sufficient funds to cover all claims. November 3, 2026 Share Article: Reported by Zdravko Ljubas OCCRP Related Articles 1 month ago Instead of Helping Ukraine’s Poor, Ferrexpo’s Charitable Donations Flowed to Companies Associated With Its Then-CEO Corruption Ukraine 3 months ago OpenLux French Prosecutors Seek Trial for BNP Paribas in Case Against Late Gabon Leader's Family Enablers Gabon 4 months ago OpenLux Son of Late Gabonese Leader Bought Sprawling Real Estate Portfolio While in Public Office Corruption Gabon 1 month ago Instead of Helping Ukraine’s Poor, Ferrexpo’s Charitable Donations Flowed to Companies Associated With Its Then-CEO Corruption Ukraine 3 months ago OpenLux French Prosecutors Seek Trial for BNP Paribas in Case Against Late Gabon Leader's Family Enablers Gabon 4 months ago OpenLux Son of Late Gabonese Leader Bought Sprawling Real Estate Portfolio While in Public Office Corruption Gabon Help us improve the website! Click below to give us input. It’ll only take 5 minutes. 👉 Help OCCRP Don't show this anymore",
