      "contentType": "text/html; charset=UTF-8",
      "pageTitle": "Today’s News For Tomorrow - Poynter",
      "pageDescription": "Apply now to join 300 newsrooms receiving free digital preservation training and services through this Press Forward-funded collaboration between Poynter, IRE, and Internet Archive. Participants gain live and asynchronous training, complimentary access to Internet Archive&#039;s professional archiving tools, the ability to share content through a local news portal and invitations to preservation-focused networking and knowledge-sharing. Cohorts begin March 2026 — secure your community&#039;s historical record before it’s lost.",
      "evidenceText": "Today’s News For Tomorrow - Poynter Today’s News For Tomorrow - Poynter DONATE What do you want to search? Submit Sign In | Membership News Training Ethics Fact-Checking Media Literacy AI Events Future of Public Media Newmark Ethics Center IFCN MediaWise PolitiFact Donate About Us Press Freedom Watch The Poynter 50 Podcasts Artificial Intelligence Business & Work Reporting & Editing Fact-Checking Ethics & Trust Local News Journalism, Media & Communication Training Poynter Membership Categories Consulting & Coaching For ACES Members For Local News Funders All Categories Broadcast & Visual Journalism Business & Work Ethics Fact-Checking & Media Literacy Leadership Reporting & Editing Writing Poynter ACES Introductory Certificate in Editing Poynter ACES Intermediate Certificate in Editing Poynter ACES Advanced Certificate in Editing Workshops and Coaching Tools for Funders Ethics Ethics Training Ethics Articles AI Ethics Public Editor Project Get Ethics Advice Fact-Checking Articles IFCN IFCN Grants International Fact-Checking Day Teen Creator Network PolitiFact MediaWise Media Literacy Resources AI Literacy Ambassadors Teen Creator Network Research & News International en Español MediaWise Training All Events The 2026 Poynter Journalism Prizes Oops! We could not locate your form. Sign In Register Sign into your account Sign In or Register to Enroll - OR - Checkout as a Guest Remember Me Reset Password Don't have an account? Register Sign In Register Register a new account Sign In or Register to Enroll Password should contain at least: Eight characters One uppercase letter One lowercase letter One number One of the following special characters: @ # - _ $ * % ^ & + = § ! ? Already have an account? Sign In Sign In Register Today’s News For Tomorrow $ 0.00 Today’s News For Tomorrow quantity Apply now Reporting & Editing Online Today’s News For Tomorrow Apply to join 300 newsrooms receiving free digital preservation training and Internet Archive services through this Press Forward-funded collaboration between Poynter, IRE, and Internet Archive. Cohorts begin March 2026. Secure professional archiving tools, expert training and peer networking to protect your community’s stories before they’re lost. March 10, 2026 &ndash; June 15, 2027 Overview The deadline for applications for the next cohort is December 11, 2026. Apply to join 300 newsrooms that will receive free digital preservation training. Engage in live and asynchronous training. Receive free access to Internet Archive’s digital preservation and web archiving services. Share local news resources through a local news access portal. Get invited to knowledge sharing and networking opportunities around local news preservation. Cohorts will begin in March, May, and September 2026 and January, April, and June 2027. There is no cost associated with participation. While this application may be completed by the participating individual, approval from an editor is required. Today’s News For Tomorrow quantity Apply now Tags: Internet Archive , Kristen Hare , Online group seminar Learning Outcomes In this program you will: Get free access to tools and services to support the preservation of digital news. Receive training on Internet Archive’s digital preservation and web archiving services. Take part in knowledge sharing and networking opportunities around local news preservation. Understand how to archive and preserve your work. Join a network of 300 stations optimizing how they archive their work. Free Apply by Dec 11, 2026 $ 0.00 Today’s News For Tomorrow quantity Apply now Overview The deadline for applications for the next cohort is December 11, 2026. Apply to join 300 newsrooms that will receive free digital preservation training. Engage in live and asynchronous training. Receive free access to Internet Archive’s digital preservation and web archiving services. Share local news resources through a local news access portal. Get invited to knowledge sharing and networking opportunities around local news preservation. Cohorts will begin in March, May, and September 2026 and January, April, and June 2027. There is no cost associated with participation. While this application may be completed by the participating individual, approval from an editor is required. Training 5 or More People? get consulting & coaching Description Schedule Requirements Content deletion. Link rot. Server disconnections. Site moves. Digital decay. The threads that chronicle the stories of your community could vanish without a trace. A 2019 study from the Reynolds Journalism Institute found that “the majority of news outlets had not given any thought to even basic strategies for preserving their digital content.” Loss of these information sources will have major consequences for future journalists, scholars, and citizens. Get the tools to archive the first draft of history. Today’s News for Tomorrow, a collaboration between the Internet Archive, Investigative Reporters and Editors (IRE), and the Poynter Institute with support from Press Forward, offers newsrooms free training, digital preservation services and sustained support to ensure the long-term preservation and access of their digital assets. Apply now for a chance to receive: Live and asynchronous training Free access to Internet Archive’s digital preservation and web archiving services Local news resources through a local news access portal An invitation to participate in knowledge sharing and networking opportunities around local news preservation Applicants will be sorted into cohorts based on geographic location, media type, or other characteristics. Cohorts will begin in March, May, and September 2026 and January, April, and June 2027. Take part in this program to preserve vital local online news that often goes offline when a publication ceases to exist. This program is provided at no cost thanks to support from Press Forward. Questions? Contact us at info@poynter.org . Appy to join newsrooms receiving digital preservation training. Applicants will be sorted into cohorts based on geographic location, media type, or other characteristics. Cohorts will begin in March, May, and September 2026 and January, April, and June 2027. The various cohorts will gather on the following dates, from 1- 3 p.m. Eastern: Cohort 1: March 10, March 24, April 7, 2026 Cohort 2: May 5, May 19, June 2, 2026 Cohort 3: September 1, September 15, September 29, 2026 Cohort 4: January 12, January 26, February 9, 2027 Cohort 5: April 6, April 20, May 4, 2027 Cohort 6: May 18, June 1, June 15, 2027 Who should apply Participants part of a media organization (from non-profit and corporate media, to public media, digital startups, college, print and broadcast media) that produces content requiring preservation. If accepted, participating organizations will be expected to have at least one representative engage in both asynchronous and live training activities. Participants will be provided with free access to tools and services to support the preservation of digital news. Application process Fill out this application . While it may be completed by the individual intending to participate in the training, approval from an editor, director, or equivalent is required. Cost There is no cost associated with participation thanks to a collaboration between the Internet Archive, Investigative Reporters and Editors (IRE), and the Poynter Institute with support from Press Forward. Instructors Kristen Hare Faculty, Director of Craft and Local News, The Poynter Institute Kristen Hare is the Poynter faculty’s director of craft and local news and the author of Local Edition, Poynter’s weekly newsletter for and about local journalists.... Read More Laura Moscoso Training Director, Investigative Reporters and Editors Laura Moscoso is a journalist and educator focusing on data and media literacy. She has been a Training Director for Investigative Reporters and Editors since... Read More Anna Trammell Community Archiving Program Manager, Internet Archive Anna Trammell is the Community Archiving Program Manager at the Internet Archive where she manages collaborative programs focused on preserving and providing access to local... Read More Thank you to our partners Thank you to our sponsor \"> Related Products Free Online Covering Child Welfare: A Journalist’s Guide to Impactful Reporting $ 0.00 Certificate Online The Art and Science of Editing $ 50.00 Sale Online Journalism Fundamentals: Craft & Values $ 50.00 Original price was: $50.00. $ 10.00 Current price is: $10.00. Sale Apply by Jul 9, 2027 Online Lead with Influence (September 2027) $ 599.00 Original price was: $599.00. $ 499.00 Current price is: $499.00.",
