      "id": "c39b098a9f9e2278d8fb51d1d78e3816ced103c7aadcae3253c3f9449d94cfbd",
      "title": "How ordinary people build solutions",
      "url": "https://us.list-manage.com/15Uk0NIF_UH",
      "category": "uncategorized",
      "checkedAt": "2026-11-10T20:53:48.541Z",
      "httpStatus": 200,
      "finalUrl": "https://nextcity.org/urbanist-news/detroits-seniors-joined-a-time-bank-for-help-became-its-biggest-helpers",
      "contentType": "text/html; charset=UTF-8",
      "pageTitle": "Detroit&#8217;s Seniors Joined a Time Bank For Help. They Became Its Biggest Helpers.",
      "pageDescription": "Time banks let members trade hours instead of dollars &mdash; and give older adults a way to contribute and receive assistance. Here&rsquo;s how cities can help support these networks.",
      "evidenceText": "Detroit’s Seniors Joined a Time Bank For Help. They Became Its Biggest Helpers. Menu Solutions for Equitable Cities Login Join Check out Next City 's new podcast and essay series, Not My Narrative . Become A Member Donate Login FAQ Institutional Membership Newsletter Upcoming Events Webinar Library Podcast Ebooks Magazine Higher Education Directory Features Latest News Economic Justice & Inclusive Finance Housing & Homelessness Transportation & Infrastructure Arts & Culture Health & Safety Environment & Energy Technology Topics Displacement Poverty Infrastructure Immigration Community Engagement Vanguard Apply for Vanguard Vanguard FAQ Vanguards Directory Host Vanguard Sponsor Vanguard Alumni Resources Shop Jobs Post a Job Find a Job About Next City Contact Advertise Pitch a Story Press Room &times; Home Urbanist News Arts And Culture Detroit’s Seniors Joined a Time Bank For Help. They Became Its Biggest Helpers. Detroit’s Seniors Joined a Time Bank For Help. They Became Its Biggest Helpers. Time banks let members trade hours instead of dollars &mdash; and give older adults a way to contribute and receive assistance. Here&rsquo;s how cities can help support these networks. Jen Zamzow   October 6, 2026 Share Facebook Twitter LinkedIn Message Email Time bank members helping fix a senior's porch in Southwest Detroit. (Photo courtesy of Unity in Our Community TimeBank) This is your first of three free stories this month. Become a free or sustaining member to read unlimited articles, webinars and ebooks. Become A Member “It is easier to pick up a phone and get a ride to the airport than ask your neighbor,” says Krista Wyatt, executive director and CEO of TimeBanks.Org . Paying for a service is simply less fraught than asking for a favor — no one wants to be a burden, and plenty of people don’t know their neighbors well enough to try. But many older adults can’t afford to pay out of pocket every time they need a ride, repair, errand, or check-in. In Detroit, n early 55% of seniors live alone ; many live in older homes in need of repairs , face transportation issues , or live with a disability . The City of Detroit already offers senior housing, roof repair, transportation, and recreation programs , but it’s harder to build a government program around the ordinary, day-to-day needs that come up for seniors: a ride to a doctor’s appointment, a porch step that’s come loose. That help still runs on informal networks, and those only work if someone willing is also available — and if asking doesn’t feel like an imposition. The Unity in Our Community TimeBank in Southwest Detroit is one of hundreds of time banks across the country helping older residents make those connections. Founded in 2009, the Unity in Our Community TimeBank has 842 members; in the last year, they’ve engaged in about 1,000 exchanges. Members earn credits by helping others, and they use those credits when they need help themselves. Members help each other with things like rides, yard work, errands, small home repairs, and sewing. No matter what the task is, everyone’s time is valued the same. Since credits carry over, members can help when they’re able and use those credits when they need something. Where seniors can serve The Unity in Our Community TimeBank was formed after two time banks in nearby neighborhoods merged. One of the two was housed within an elder-serving organization that wanted another way to meet seniors’ needs outside its existing services. But coordinator Alice Bagley says older adults quickly became some of the time bank’s most active contributors: They make calls, share knowledge, repair things, offer companionship, and mentor younger neighbors. Across the country, in Northern California, the Sebastopol Area Time Bank sees a similar dynamic. Coordinator David Gill says the time bank facilitates about 45 exchanges each month, with thousands since our founding about a decade ago. One of its most active members is Jim, a retired widower in his late 80s who “loves working with his hands,” according to Gill. “He’s got incredible skills and knowledge, but he didn’t have a mechanism to be able to use them,” Gill says. The time bank gave him one. Since joining, Jim has logged more than 350 hours helping others: “He’s helping an incredible number of people and sharing his personality. And he’s found a home for himself. He’s found purpose.” Sebastopol Area Time Bank member Patricia shows off her new curtains, made by another member. (Photo courtesy of Sebastopol Area Time Bank) For older adults, being able to contribute and remain part of community life can be especially valuable since more people are aging alone and one in three older adults reports feeling lonely or socially isolated. Purpose , connection , and a sense of mattering are all linked with flourishing later in life. Recognizing that value, the city provided early funding to help incubate the Sebastopol time bank in 2015, and it’s now fiscally sponsored by the Sebastopol Community Cultural Center. The time bank also partners with the Sebastopol Area Senior Center: Volunteers can earn time-bank credits for the hours they serve at the Senior Center and they can use those credits elsewhere in the network. The partnership gives the time bank a way to reach new members, while “the Senior Center gets to reward volunteers in a way that they’ve never been able to before,” says Gill. The time banking model Time banking has been around for more than 40 years in the United States. Civil rights attorney Edgar Cahn began developing what he called “Time Dollars” in the early ‘80s, following the Reagan administration slashing funding for social welfare programs. Cahn later founded the Time Dollar Institute, which eventually became TimeBanks.Org. Cahn saw time banking as a way to value caregiving and community work that’s often unpaid and underappreciated. He argued that there is a wealth of human talent that the market ignores, and that people need more opportunities to contribute — and to be recognized as contributors. Communities have since adapted his model in different ways. Some time banks operate as standalone community exchanges. Others are housed inside senior centers, churches, healthcare networks, and nonprofits that use the model to coordinate support among the people they already serve. Neighborly help doesn’t organize itself Time banking can’t fill all the gaps in elder care. Members don’t handle medical care, personal care, or other needs that require trained professionals. And even requests that neighbors could manage may not be met if the demand outpaces the available help. Sebastopol Area Time Bank members Wee and Michael learn to make croissants. (Photo courtesy of Sebastopol Area Time Bank) In Southwest Detroit, Bagley says home repairs are consistently in high demand, in part because many of the city’s homes are older and it can be hard to keep up with maintenance and repairs. Matching requests with the right person can take time, too, Bagley says. A time bank can give people a wider circle of support, but it still may not be as quick or convenient as hiring someone. A time bank is only effective when it has active members, so that someone is available when a request comes in. Keeping that going requires dedicated work, though: Someone has to recruit participants, connect requests with offers, help new members get started, maintain partnerships, and keep people participating. Building trust is part of that work. Bagley describes the time bank as “ultimately a trust-based system” and said that “trust has to be earned.” If someone asks for a ride and the person shows up, they’ll become more comfortable relying on the network. When a new member posts an offer, Bagley encourages other members to take them up on it so the newcomer can get involved and build confidence in the system. This coordination can be hard for volunteers to sustain on their own. How can cities and institutions help? Wyatt, from the national time banks network TimeBanks.Org, says coordinators can “get burned out pretty fast” without enough support. As in Sebastopol, cities and community organizations can help by funding coordination, connecting time banks with people and organizations they already serve, or providing space, training, and administrative support, she says. Time banking doesn’t fit the standard categories institutions often use to support programs. Bagley says the Unity in Our Community TimeBank has been able to get some support for specific activities, like a yoga class that could qualify for an active-lifestyle grant or a cooking class that could qualify for a nutrition grant. But it’s been harder to get funding for the exchange network itself. “It’s not a concept that fits in any boxes,” Bagley says. While funding and institutional support can give a time bank resources, the exchange still hinges on members actively participating, she notes. Through her work with the Michigan Alliance of TimeBanks , Bagley knew of a time bank in Pontiac that received initial institutional support and funding but had less involvement from community members. When the funding period ended, “there just wasn’t a lot left,” she says. Part of the funding challenge is that much of what members gain from a time bank is hard to quantify. It’s easy to count things like hours and exchanges. It’s harder to measure whether those exchanges helped someone feel more useful or supported or whether the network helped a community come together. Not only are these benefits hard to measure, they’re also hard to generate. Chao Guo, a University of Pennsylvania researcher studying time banking , noted that care and other forms of relationship-based support are private goods that markets often struggle to provide well. Time banking gives people another way to provide this support — and to get something in return. Guo said time banks can also create public goods by strengthening community ties and a sense of belonging. As more services are delivered remotely or through digital platforms, time banks help keep at least some forms of care rooted in local relationships. The way time credits function can also keep more of that value local. As Cahn wrote in the book No More Throw-Away People , “Money knows no geographic loyalty.” Money earned for a task can be spent anywhere. When credits can only be exchanged for local activities or help from other members, the value created by members grows within the community. That gives cities another way to help with the everyday needs that fall outside existing programs. They can’t create a program for every need older adults might have, but they can support the local networks that help residents find and help one another. Support for this reporting came from the Better Life Lab at New America and from UC Berkeley’s Greater Good Science Center, as part of its “Spreading Love Through the Media” initiative, supported by the John Templeton Foundation. Prove It’s Possible When the naysayers doubt it can be done, send them our story about a real solution.  Next City isn&rsquo;t like other media. We believe change is happening&mdash;and we make it our job to find it. As a nonprofit, we&rsquo;ve relied on reader support since 2003. Nearly 2,000 people give each year to ensure this reporting remains freely available to hundreds of thousands working toward more equitable cities. Donate Now Like what you&rsquo;re reading? Get a browser notification whenever we post a new story. You&rsquo;re signed-up for browser notifications of new stories. No longer want to be notified? Unsubscribe.  Jen Zamzow, PhD, is an adjunct professor of healthcare ethics at Concordia University Irvine, a writer, and a mom to two young boys. She writes  the newsletter &ldquo; A Well-Lived Life .&rdquo; Tags: seniors Share Facebook X/Twitter LinkedIn Message Email Comments Add to the Discussion Next City sustaining members can comment on our stories. Keep the discussion 